{
  "name": "Schrödinger NAND gates",
  "description": "Four NAND gates made only of colliding solitons, no walls: one per input pair, so the truth table plays out at once (top left A=0 B=0, top right 0 1, bottom left 1 0, bottom right 1 1). A bit is a soliton present (1) or absent (0). Inputs A (from lower left) and B (upper left) are sent in phase on paths 30° apart: alone, each carries on; together they attract and merge into one heavy soliton heading east (that is A AND B). The slow clock soliton (lower right, always sent) crosses that path: if the merged soliton is there it swallows the clock, otherwise the clock goes on into the output box (NOT). So the box gets a soliton unless both inputs are 1: NAND, which can build any logic. The clocks reach the boxes after about 14742 steps (20 s at 12 steps per frame and 60 fps). Each box holds a trap (orange circle) that catches the output soliton and keeps it there at the standard power, so the answer stays: a soliton sitting in the box is a 1, an empty box a 0 (the boxes are probes, holding their highest reading too). In the lab a single gate gave the same answers with the clock up to 600 steps early or late, and at any clock phase. Built on Fredkin and Toffoli's billiard-ball computer (1982), with solitons for balls. Everything outside the gates' paths is absorbing (red): a lone input once past P1, the merged soliton after the swallow and any stray fragment are soaked up wherever they go, so nothing wraps round into the other gates or wanders into a trap. The faint blue band before each trap is a low bump in the potential: the clock has the speed to cross it, the slow heavy remnant of a swallow does not and bounces back. Try: pause at the start and erase an input (or the clock) to see that gate change its answer.",
